          "id": "ux-product-page-audit",
          "name": "UX Product Page Audit",
          "text": "<skill_content name=\"UX Product Page Audit\">\n# Skill: UX Product Page Audit\n\n# UX Product Page Audit & Redesign\n\nA systematic pass over a product detail page (PDP) — or any screen dense with content —\nderived from a full teardown of a real e-commerce redesign.\n\nThe method has three phases, in order:\n\n1. **Find the mistakes** (15-point audit, below)\n2. **Fix them** (do not add polish until the audit is clean)\n3. **Raise the ceiling** (3 structural UX improvements)\n\nNever skip to phase 3. A page that is still broken cannot be made good by cleverness.\n\nReference: `references/audit-checklist.md` holds the full 15-item checklist in\ntickable form. Read it before starting the audit.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1 — The 15-point audit\n\nRun every item even if the screen \"looks fine\". Most failures are only visible\nonce you test the design against a *different* image, product, or data set.\n\n### A. Robustness (does it survive real content?)\n\n1. **Icons over imagery** — Put navigation/control icons on top of the product\n   image? Swap the image for a bright/white/busy one. If the icons lose contrast,\n   they were never designed — they were only *accidentally* visible. Fix: give\n   icon buttons a subtle background container, sufficient contrast, and a thin\n   outline so they read on any background.\n2. **Product image choice** — Score candidates on four axes, in this order:\n   - *Focal point*: is the product the subject, or the hands/background/props?\n   - *Honesty*: does the image match what is actually sold (unit, count, weight)?\n   - *Naturalness*: for organic/fresh/handmade goods, avoid artificial tints\n     (a pink backdrop on strawberries reads as fake).\n   - *System fit*: the image will sit in a grid next to 50 others. If every card\n     has its own background, lighting and props, the catalog looks messy.\n     Prefer: clean background, natural product, uniform visual system.\n   - **Rule: a good product image works alone AND inside the full grid.**\n\n### B. Structure\n\n3. **Alignment** — Pick one content margin (e.g. 24px) and enforce it everywhere:\n   text, icons, prices, buttons, section edges. Misalignment is felt, not seen —\n   the screen becomes \"less calm, less structured, less trustworthy\".\n4. **Color** — Too bright / too saturated / too many competing accents?\n   Saturation should emphasize the *product*, not the chrome. Fix: softer, more\n   natural palette; color sets mood and supports the product, it does not shout.\n5. **Typography** — Too many font styles? One strong family is enough.\n   Build hierarchy from **size, weight, color, line-height** — not from more fonts.\n6. **Labels & badges** — Small uppercase labels need letter-spacing to breathe.\n   Badges must be scannable in one glance: `20% off`, not `20% off discount`\n   plus an icon. Cut words until the badge reads instantly.\n\n### C. Hierarchy of information\n\n7. **The title** — Must stand out without shouting. Adjust size and weight until\n   it is clearly the top of the reading order but balanced with the layout.\n8. **Paragraph text** — Increase line-height, slightly reduce body contrast.\n   Rule of thumb: **headlines attract attention, paragraphs support understanding.**\n9. **Review placement** — Ratings are one of the strongest trust signals: they\n   must sit *next to the title*, never a screen away. Proximity to the name lets\n   the user answer three questions at once — what is it, how is it rated, do\n   others trust it.\n10. **Feature icons (benefit row)** — Typical failures: too many colors, mixed\n    icon styles (filled vs outlined, heavy vs light), loose spacing. Fix: one\n    icon weight, one color logic, tighter spacing. Color variety is allowed only\n    when intentional.\n11. **Divider lines** — Dark, heavy rules chop the page into harsh blocks.\n    Soften them until they separate without interrupting. Small details are what\n    make an interface feel premium.\n12. **Spacing balance** — White space is not automatically good. Too much in the\n    wrong place disconnects sections. Tighten until the flow scans as one\n    experience. Good spacing = the right *relationship* between elements.\n\n### D. The buying decision\n\n13. **Redundant labels** — A `Price` label before `$4.50` is noise: the currency\n    symbol already says it. Either label *everything* (then the quantity\n    selector needs a label too, for consistency) or label *nothing* and let the\n    price speak. Remove it.\n14. **Quantity in the title** — Never bake a mutable value (\"1 kilogram\") into\n    the product title when the user can change it with a selector; the title\n    becomes a lie. Title = the product itself. Quantity belongs to the buying\n    area. Also check **price position**: users should learn *what it is, how good\n    it is, and what it costs* early — move the price up when the audience\n    decides on price.\n15. **Primary action** — Add-to-cart is the most important element on screen;\n    if it is ALL-CAPS, oversized and shouting, tone it down to feel refined.\n    Then fix the mechanics:\n    - quantity selector must sit **directly next to** add-to-cart\n      (choose-amount → add, in one eye movement),\n    - put the unit inside the selector (`kg`), not in the title,\n    - put the **total price inside the button** so the user knows what they\n      will pay before clicking — this removes uncertainty.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2 — Apply the fixes\n\n- Fix in the order of the checklist (robustness → structure → hierarchy →\n  conversion). Later fixes depend on earlier ones (you cannot judge spacing on a\n  misaligned grid).\n- After each group, re-check the page against **different data**: a bright\n  product image, a long product name, a 3-digit price, a missing rating, a\n  0%-off state. Design for the system, not for the one screenshot.\n- Keep a \"before\" reference so the delta is visible.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 3 — Raise the ceiling (structural improvements)\n\nOnly after the audit is clean. These change the *experience*, not just the pixels.\n\n1. **Scrollable card over hero** — Turn the content area into a soft card with\n   rounded corners and a subtle shadow that slides up over the product image as\n   the user scrolls. The image owns the first screen; then the **product title\n   transitions into a sticky top bar** so context is never lost deeper down.\n2. **Sticky bottom action bar** — Keep quantity + add-to-cart visible while\n   scrolling. Users do not always decide at the top; they read, compare, then\n   act — and when ready, the action must be right there.\n3. **Predefined quantity options** — Offer `500 g / 1 kg / 2 kg` one-tap chips\n   (driven by real purchase data: surface what most customers buy), while\n   keeping the custom stepper for flexibility. Small change, direct business\n   impact.\n\n### Done criteria\n\n- [ ] Every one of the 15 checks passes, tested against a *second* product image\n      and longer/shorter content.\n- [ ] One grid, one margin, one icon weight, one font family.\n- [ ] Title → rating → price → action read top-to-bottom with no detours.\n- [ ] Price is visible early, and repeated in the button as a total.\n- [ ] Primary action reachable without scrolling (sticky bar) and calm in tone.\n\n---\n\n## Working with code/UI in this repo\n\nWhen implementing, keep the existing stack's conventions: reuse existing\ncomponents for buttons, badges, steppers and dividers rather than introducing\nnew ones, and express the fixes with the project's current spacing/color tokens\nif they exist (create a token only if the project has a token system).\n\nBase directory for this skill: C:\\Users\\nitin\\OneDrive\\Documents\\Default Project\\.opencode\\skills\\ux-product-page-audit\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>C:\\Users\\nitin\\OneDrive\\Documents\\Default Project\\.opencode\\skills